#%PAM-1.0
# W Linux — polkit authentication stack (see quickshell-auth.md).
#
# Fingerprint first (a no-op if fprintd is not configured or no finger is
# enrolled), then fall through to password via system-auth.
#
# The first line exists so the SECOND one can be skipped on demand. While the W
# auth card waits on the reader it offers "Use password"; pam_fprintd owns the
# conversation until its own max-tries/timeout expire (30s by default), so
# without this the button could only reveal the field and let the typed password
# sit queued. GDM solves this by having two PAM services and choosing one —
# polkit cannot, because polkit-agent-helper-1 hardcodes the service name
# "polkit-1", so the choice has to be made inside this single stack.
#
#   gate exit 0     → success=ignore → continue here, pam_fprintd runs as always
#   gate exit != 0  → default=1      → jump over pam_fprintd → system-auth
#
# Every failure of the gate (missing, unreadable, not executable) also lands on
# `default`, i.e. on "ask for the password" — it can never skip authentication.
# See /usr/lib/w/w-fp-gate for the flag it reads and why it is safe.
auth       [success=ignore default=1]   pam_exec.so quiet /usr/lib/w/w-fp-gate
auth       sufficient   pam_fprintd.so
auth       include      system-auth
account    include      system-auth
session    include      system-auth
